<p>An access boundary says what someone can do with the database, and what prevents them doing more. A password is only one part. An application may have the right password but no route to the server; a reporter may connect successfully and still be unable to read customer addresses. Someone who can download a backup may read those addresses without making a SQL connection at all.</p>
<p>Follow the shop from <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a>. The Order API changes orders, a deployment job changes their schema and staff report on order status. Mina’s order 101 contains an address that reports do not need. We will make those different jobs visible in the access design, verify both useful and forbidden work, then retire a credential suspected of being stolen.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL 18 supplies the concrete contract. A disposable PostgreSQL 18.6 run checked the selected paths through node-postgres 8.23.1 and <code>psql</code>. The figures are explanatory models, not live security tests. For the final compromise exercise, first read the parent’s <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#recover-the-incident-without-destroying-the-evidence">incident decision method</a>; it owns command, evidence preservation and the return-to-service decision.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article" class="article-contents"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="turn-access-needs-into-testable-boundaries">Turn access needs into testable boundaries</h2>
<p>Start with a task and name its opposite. “The API can update an order” needs the companion test “the API cannot change the schema or delegate its authority”. Record the identity, route, objects, operations and duration together. Otherwise a narrowly granted database login can conceal a broadly privileged deployment key used by the same process.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The shop’s access map"><table><thead><tr><th>Job and duration</th><th>Intended route and authority</th><th>Boundary to prove</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Order API, while deployed</td><td>Application route; read, insert and update order data as <code>order_work</code></td><td>No ownership, schema changes, role delegation or backup/control-plane credentials</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Deployment, approved change window</td><td>Separate runner and login; explicitly become <code>shop_owner</code></td><td>Ordinary app cannot use this path; deployment access ends after the job</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Reporting, authorised staff session</td><td>Reporting route; approved status projection</td><td>No underlying addresses, writes or grant authority</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Backup and recovery, scheduled or approved work</td><td>Tool-specific database access; separate copy store and key permissions</td><td>Application cannot retrieve/delete copies; recovery still works without production access</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Emergency operator, short approved interval</td><td>Separately controlled identity and route; attributable necessary powers</td><td>No routine use; activation, work and retirement leave evidence</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>This is an intended design, not five portable SQL roles. A physical backup identity and a logical export identity need different powers. A managed snapshot may be governed entirely through the provider API. Map database files, host administration, replicas, exports, snapshots and archived logs too: each can expose data or remove a recovery option.</p>
<p>Use the parent’s <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#assign-every-operating-responsibility">responsibility map</a> to put an owner beside each boundary. The provider may maintain the host and encrypt storage; the shop still chooses who can change network admission, restore snapshots, retrieve secrets and use keys. Check those rights in the actual account and service, rather than inferring them from the SQL role list.</p>

<h2 id="admit-only-the-intended-connections">Admit only the intended connections</h2>
<p>A connection first needs an address and route to a listening server. Prefer the intended private path; explicitly restrict any exposed endpoint. Include operator, deployment, monitoring, backup and replica paths in the same inventory. Test from a representative permitted workload and a forbidden location. A laptop on loopback cannot establish what a production firewall admits.</p>
<p>Next comes server admission and <strong>authentication</strong>: establishing the identity opening the session. Only then can the server decide whether that identity may use a particular object. Figure 1 follows a reporting connection and compares its two queries. At the last step, change the route or password and see where work stops.</p>
<SecurityFigure scene="admission" number={1}/>
<p>PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/auth-pg-hba-conf.html"><code>pg_hba.conf</code></a> uses the first rule matching connection type, client address, database and user. Failed authentication does not try a later rule. A broad earlier rule can therefore defeat a carefully written later restriction. Inspect parsed rules and errors in <code>pg_hba_file_rules</code>, reload through the supported procedure, then make fresh connections to establish the result.</p>
<p>A <code>hostssl</code> rule matches TLS TCP connections; a <code>host</code> rule can also match plaintext. The drill explicitly rejected non-TLS loopback TCP, required SCRAM password authentication for selected users and separately rejected other users on the Unix socket. It allowed only the private local provisioner to use socket trust. Those rules are fixture configuration, not a production template.</p>
<p>Local sockets have their own admission rules and filesystem permissions; host or database-file administrators may bypass the SQL boundary altogether. Inspect IPv4, IPv6, proxies and provider endpoints that exist in the deployment. Verify the effective database user after any proxy or identity mapping, and check the database’s <code>CONNECT</code> grant as well as authentication. The native outsider had a valid password and matching TLS admission rule but was refused database access.</p>

<h2 id="give-each-job-the-authority-it-needs">Give each job the authority it needs</h2>
<p>Separate a <strong>login identity</strong>, whose credential a consumer holds, from a reusable <strong>privilege role</strong>, which collects the work it may perform. PostgreSQL calls both roles; <code>LOGIN</code> makes one usable for authentication. Here <code>app_old</code> inherits <code>order_work</code>, while a migration login must deliberately <code>SET ROLE shop_owner</code>. Never give the API membership in the owner role just to make a deployment pass.</p>
<Code code={grants} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>This is deliberately PostgreSQL 18 syntax. Membership options distinguish automatic use through <code>INHERIT</code>, becoming a role through <code>SET</code> and delegating membership through <code>ADMIN</code>. Object <code>GRANT OPTION</code> is a separate authority to pass on object privileges. Administrative attributes such as <code>SUPERUSER</code>, <code>CREATEROLE</code>, <code>REPLICATION</code> and <code>BYPASSRLS</code> need their own review. Follow indirect memberships too; the shortest role list is not necessarily the smallest effective authority.</p>
<p><strong>Ownership</strong> includes control over an object. Revoking a table’s ordinary write grants does not take its owner’s power to alter or drop it. Figure 2 compares an app that uses a separately owned object with an app that owns it. Advance to the DDL attempt, then change what deployment creates.</p>
<SecurityFigure scene="authority" number={2}/>
<p>The actual migration login could not create in the orders schema until it became <code>shop_owner</code>. Its new table was then owned by that role and acquired the owner’s default table grants; the application could read it. Defaults apply to future objects created by the relevant role, not retroactively to existing tables or automatically from every role the creator belongs to. Verify both existing and newly created objects after each deployment.</p>
<p>The setup also revoked database privileges and public-schema creation from <code>PUBLIC</code>, the implicit set of all roles, then granted the required database access explicitly. Existing or upgraded databases can carry different defaults. Sequences, schemas and routines have separate privileges: a granted table insert can still fail on its generated identifier. A new PostgreSQL routine normally grants <code>EXECUTE</code> to <code>PUBLIC</code>; the fixture removed that owner default before creation and observed the application’s call being denied. Remove inappropriate creation and execution defaults in the same controlled deployment that creates the objects.</p>
<p>The reporter reads an owner-defined view containing only ID and status. The fixture rejected direct access to the addresses’ schema. A view is useful here because the required boundary is a projection; its grants, owner and underlying access must all be checked. If the database must enforce row separation, use its row-security contract and test bypasses: PostgreSQL superusers and <code>BYPASSRLS</code> roles bypass policies, and table owners normally do unless forced. Application decisions about which customer may retrieve an order remain in <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#preserve-values-and-authority-when-sending-sql">application authorisation and query safety</a>.</p>
<p>Names can open another route. PostgreSQL resolves unqualified objects through <code>search_path</code>; allowing an untrusted user to create in a searched schema trusts them to influence name resolution. Schema-qualify critical references and remove unneeded creation rights. A <code>SECURITY DEFINER</code> routine executes with its owner’s authority, so treat it as an additional entry point: narrow its owner and execution grants, set a safe search path excluding untrusted writable schemas with <code>pg_temp</code> last, and test its inputs and effects. The drill denies execution of an ordinary new routine; it does not establish the safety of a production privileged routine.</p>

<h2 id="protect-connections-copies-and-the-keys-that-unlock-them">Protect connections, copies and the keys that unlock them</h2>
<p>TLS encrypts a channel. The client also needs to establish <em>which server</em> is at the other end. A certificate chain trusted by the client and a certificate valid for the expected service name provide that check under the client’s verification contract. Figure 3 compares strict verification with deliberately disabled peer verification. Try the wrong-name certificate at the same verification step.</p>
<SecurityFigure scene="tls" number={3}/>
<p>For libpq clients such as <code>psql</code>, <code>sslmode=verify-full</code> checks the certificate chain and hostname with the configured trust roots. Do not assume an encryption-only mode means that. node-postgres instead passes its <code>ssl</code> options to Node’s TLS implementation. The native fixture used this explicit configuration and observed both wrong-name and untrusted-issuer rejection:</p>
<Code code={tls} lang="javascript" themes={themes}/>
<p>Check the final driver configuration, including connection strings that can replace TLS options. Use the actual endpoint name through the proxy or service arrangement. The native <code>psql</code> check used <code>host=localhost</code> for verification and <code>hostaddr=127.0.0.1</code> for transport. These are server-certificate checks; this fixture authenticated clients with SCRAM, not client certificates. A chosen client-certificate scheme needs separate certificate-to-database-identity, expiry and revocation tests.</p>
<p>Encryption at rest answers a different question: who can read a stored copy without going through the running engine? PostgreSQL documents filesystem or block-device encryption as an option; the database process still reads plaintext while the storage is unlocked. That does not exclude an authorised SQL reader or the trusted host administrator. The local drill makes no at-rest claim.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Encryption coverage and key acceptance"><table><thead><tr><th>Copy or path</th><th>Contract to establish</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Database, WAL, temporary files</td><td>All actual volumes and spill locations covered; host and key administrators identified</td><td>Deployed encryption settings and a checked inventory of storage locations</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Backup, snapshot and replica</td><td>Encryption travels with each supported copy operation; retrieval, deletion and key rights separately controlled</td><td>Recovery identity retrieves and decrypts an approved copy without production authority</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Logical export, downloaded report, copied logs</td><td>Destination protection and retention explicitly chosen</td><td>No assumed inheritance from the source; inspect the export path and access policy</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>For a specific managed comparison, <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.Encryption.html">Amazon RDS encryption</a> covers an encrypted instance’s underlying storage, logs, automated backups, read replicas and snapshots under its documented restrictions. Customer-managed KMS key access is part of availability: disabling or losing required key access can make data unavailable. Establish replica, snapshot-copy and cross-account restrictions for the chosen configuration; do not infer protection for an arbitrary SQL export. The <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/#keep-recovery-possible-after-production-fails">restoration rehearsal</a> must prove that the required copies and keys remain usable after the named production or account failure.</p>

<h2 id="change-credentials-without-losing-control-of-access">Change credentials without losing control of access</h2>
<p>For a planned password rotation, first inventory consumers: active API pools, workers, scheduled reports, deployment runners and any backup or replication clients using that identity. Give different jobs different credentials so an API rotation does not silently break recovery. Keep secrets in an appropriately controlled delivery mechanism, away from source, command arguments, statement logs and broadly readable configuration.</p>
<p>The shop introduces <code>app_new</code> with the same narrow work role, updates consumers, reconnects them and verifies permitted work under the new login. Two logins allow a bounded overlap without pretending one PostgreSQL role accepts two passwords simultaneously. Record which consumers have actually moved, then disable the old login and deliberately drain or terminate its remaining sessions. Do not leave the old credential as a permanent fallback.</p>
<p>Figure 4 starts with a connected pool. At the final step, compare changing the password, setting NOLOGIN and also ending selected sessions.</p>
<SecurityFigure scene="rotation" number={4}/>
<p>In the native run, replacing the password rejected the old password on a new connection; the established connection still read order 101. Expiring the password also rejected a fresh password login while that session still worked. After removing expiry to test NOLOGIN separately, NOLOGIN rejected a new login and again left the session usable. PostgreSQL password <code>VALID UNTIL</code> governs password authentication, not a universal expiry of every authentication method or existing session.</p>
<p>A committed membership revocation then denied that session’s subsequent table request. It did not close the connection. The selected backend termination returned true, and a new activity observation found zero rows for that PID. Do not generalise this subsequent-statement result to cancellation of work already running, or to every role state: inspect sessions that have already changed role, outstanding transactions and the selected engine’s semantics. For suspected compromise, explicit session exclusion is part of containment.</p>
<p>A certificate renewal needs the same consumer inventory, but the server’s identity and the client’s trust are separate materials. Before a root change, distribute a bounded overlap bundle trusting the old and new issuers. Install a valid new server certificate and key through the engine or provider’s supported procedure; make fresh connections from each actual client; then remove the old trust after every relevant endpoint has moved. Track expiry with enough time for failed deployment and repair.</p>
<p>The fixture switched its server certificate to a second disposable CA using PostgreSQL reload. Fresh clients trusting only the old root failed; overlap and new-root clients passed. A TLS session established before the change still queried successfully. Root removal is therefore not session revocation. The run did not test certificate expiry, revocation lists, client-certificate authentication or a managed proxy. Follow the chosen provider’s renewal contract and repeat those checks where required.</p>
<p>A secret manager rotating the stored secret and database credential does not itself prove every consumer has fetched the new version or replaced its pool. <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html">Secrets Manager’s rotation contract</a> describes updating the secret and its target; consumer adoption and usable new connections still need evidence. Coordinate replica and backup dependencies with <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">topology operation</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">controlled maintenance</a>.</p>

<h2 id="prove-permitted-work-succeeds-and-forbidden-work-fails">Prove permitted work succeeds and forbidden work fails</h2>
<p>A catalogue inspection predicts authority. An attempted action establishes what this client and configuration actually permit. Use each intended identity through its real route, including any proxy and pool. Establish the connection’s effective identity, then exercise a meaningful allowed operation and a named forbidden one. Use disposable data or an isolated representative environment for destructive attempts.</p>
<p><a href={rehearsalUrl} download="rehearse.mjs">Download the disposable security rehearsal</a>. It requires Node.js 24, pg 8.23.1, OpenSSL and PostgreSQL 18 server tools, with an ordinary OS account allowed to start local servers. Supply the installed tool and module paths and a new evidence filename:</p>
<Code code={run} lang="sh" themes={themes}/>
<p>The harness creates a fresh private temporary cluster and unused loopback port, dummy credentials, two temporary CAs and server certificates. It contacts no existing database or external service, alters no trust store and requires no repository dependency changes. It saves its configuration, results and server log, stops its own server and removes its database and certificates. If server shutdown fails it retains its directory for controlled cleanup and records the failure. Remove the separately installed dependency directory after retaining the evidence. The fixture never uses production credentials and is not a production acceptance test.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Selected observed PostgreSQL 18.6 security outcomes"><table><thead><tr><th>Attempt through the native fixture</th><th>Observed result</th><th>Boundary established</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>API updates 101; reporter reads projection</td><td><code>packed</code> returned through both paths</td><td>Required work succeeds under separate logins</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Reporter reads raw addresses; app alters table or becomes owner</td><td>Permission denied</td><td>Selected cross-boundary data and DDL paths fail</td></tr>
 <tr><td>App grants table access or role membership</td><td>No effective table grant; membership delegation denied</td><td>A successful command response alone is insufficient: inspect the resulting privilege</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Migration creates after SET ROLE; app reads new table and calls ungranted routine</td><td>Owner <code>shop_owner</code>; table read succeeds; routine call denied</td><td>Creator defaults preserve selected new-object boundaries</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Plaintext TCP, wrong password, ungranted database CONNECT</td><td>Each rejected at its respective gate</td><td>Transport, authentication and database access are distinct</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Wrong hostname or untrusted issuer</td><td>Strict client rejects; valid path uses TLS 1.3</td><td>Selected peer-verification configuration works</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Retire password/login, revoke membership, end selected backend</td><td>New-login and existing-session results differ as described above</td><td>Retirement must check both session lifetimes</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Retain expected result, actual result, engine and driver versions, role memberships, ownership, server admission and client TLS settings with each test. Repeat after grants, new objects, migrations, upgrades, restore, network changes and rotation. Include inherited roles, sequences, privileged routines and row-policy bypasses wherever the real design uses them.</p>
<p>This fixture does not test remote network policy, provider IAM, backup-store access, encryption at rest, emergency activation or production application authorisation. Add positive and negative acceptance checks for those paths: the application cannot retrieve a snapshot or revoke a recovery key; the recovery identity can retrieve/decrypt the intended copy; a retired operator cannot reconnect through an alternate route. Run them in the actual service’s safe test environment and retain their results. An illustrative matrix or this local success cannot supply missing hosted evidence.</p>

<h2 id="leave-useful-evidence-and-a-controlled-emergency-route">Leave useful evidence and a controlled emergency route</h2>
<p>Ask what a future investigation must distinguish. A failed login, a successful session, a changed grant and a sensitive read are different events. Capture actor, effective identity, time with zone, affected object and outcome where the engine or service supports them. Add workload or change identifiers for correlation, recognising that client-supplied application names are labels, not proof of a human actor.</p>
<p>The fixture enabled connection and disconnection logging with timestamp, backend PID, user and database. Its log recorded SCRAM authentication, TLS protocol, denied connections and termination. A separate operator session briefly enabled <code>log_statement='ddl'</code> and recorded creation and removal of a dummy table. It did not enable a complete data-read audit. Ordinary PostgreSQL operational logs under these settings cannot establish every address read; WAL is a recovery history, not a complete read-access ledger.</p>
<p>If sensitive-read or privilege-change auditing is required, establish the exact engine extension or provider facility, supported events, attribution and omissions before relying on it. Test a known successful read, rejected read, role/ownership change, privileged routine and recovery-copy retrieval against the collected records. Control statement values and bind parameters: query logging can expose addresses, tokens and credentials. Confirm filtering without removing the actor and object evidence the investigation needs.</p>
<p>Assign retention and retrieval owners, protect records against the same authority that can change the data, and check that export, clock handling and alerting work. A database administrator able to edit the only log copy can also erase the explanation. Provider control-plane records and copy-store access records may be needed alongside database logs; verify their own retention and permissions.</p>
<p>The emergency route is a separate capability, activated for a named person and purpose for a short interval. Keep its credential and activation authority away from the ordinary application, require attributable approval and record use in independently protected evidence. Test activation, the necessary repair or diagnostic work, fresh login denial after retirement and closure of remaining sessions. Expiring a stored password alone does not close that exercise.</p>
<p>Also rehearse the dependency failure: can an authorised operator obtain the necessary access if the normal identity service, secret store or provider control plane is unavailable? Use the service’s supported recovery path, with protected custody and review. An undocumented permanent administrator password in the application is not a tested emergency route. This local drill does not establish the shop’s emergency-access contract.</p>

<h2 id="revoke-compromised-access-and-verify-the-boundary-again">Revoke compromised access and verify the boundary again</h2>
<p>Suppose <code>app_old</code> may have been stolen. Apply the parent’s <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#recover-the-incident-without-destroying-the-evidence">incident method</a> to this boundary: identify the affected service and owner, preserve available evidence while containing continuing exposure, then select the supported access-recovery procedure. Include every consumer and route that can use this identity and any secret-store or provider authority that may also be affected.</p>
<p>Figure 5 compares the boundary now with what can be known about earlier damage. Change the surviving audit coverage at the final step. The old session is closed in both cases.</p>
<SecurityFigure scene="incident" number={5}/>
<p>Restrict the affected route where that safely limits continuing harm. Preserve useful activity, connection, grant and deployment records; record the containment changes and times. Disable the compromised authentication path, replace exposed material through a trusted path and end the identified sessions under verified engine procedures. The actual app had order-data authority but lacked schema ownership and delegation; investigate against those real powers rather than assuming either no harm or total administrator control.</p>
<Code code={retire} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>Termination can interrupt transactions and leave callers uncertain about completed work; coordinate with the <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#recover-according-to-what-the-application-knows">application recovery owner</a>. A reviewed PID identifies one observed backend, not every proxy connection or session created after the inventory. Block new admission before enumeration, recheck activity and all relevant endpoints, and verify the retired credential fails from an intended client.</p>
<p>Inspect actual memberships, grants, owners, unexpected objects and privileged execution paths, scheduled work, exports and copy-store activity according to the affected identity’s authority. Compare orders and dependent facts with trusted application records. If a broader deployment or administrator identity was exposed, enlarge that investigation accordingly. Revocation stops further tested access; rotation cannot recover disclosed addresses, undo a committed update or prove that no copy was taken.</p>
<p>Keep affected traffic excluded if integrity remains uncertain. Hand possible reconstruction and validation to <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">the restoration owner</a>, preserving current state and valid later work. The return gate requires trustworthy replacement credentials and routes, fresh permitted application work, all named forbidden attempts still rejected, affected sessions excluded and the application owner’s acceptance of the relevant facts. Record remaining uncertainty, including reads the retained evidence cannot establish. Then repeat the boundary tests after the control repair, keeping the result inside the common incident decision record.</p>

